  "Question": "Solve \\((2x-3)²=(x+1)²\\). (Separate the two roots with a comma, writing them as integers or fractions in lowest terms.)",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4，2/3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": true,
  "Solution": "Taking square roots on both sides gives \\(2x-3=x+1\\) or \\(2x-3=-(x+1)\\). The former gives \\(x=4\\); the latter gives \\(3x=2\\), so \\(x=2/3\\). Alternatively, move terms and factor using the difference of squares.\n\nAlternative method 1: Factor a difference of squares\n\nSubtract the squared expressions so that the difference-of-squares identity produces linear factors:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x-3)^2-(x+1)^2&=0,\\\\\n[(2x-3)-(x+1)][(2x-3)+(x+1)]&=0,\\\\\n(x-4)(3x-2)&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nA product is zero if and only if at least one factor is zero. Thus \\(x-4=0\\) or \\(3x-2=0\\), giving the two roots \\(4,\\,2/3\\).",
